#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571482} :activity/id #uuid "29b930e0-d300-5c18-b22e-b6c8fa5110b7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/id #uuid "c29ac9c3-f2c1-572b-9251-f82101c5a30e" :topic/math-academy-id 1375} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571477 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4537924c-6e29-55ff-b3f8-dc181ec0e42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570995} {:answer/id #uuid "b0aecb63-c63a-50f2-a367-8c0bc46f3d0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571164} {:answer/id #uuid "ba493e6d-d0cf-5095-bfa8-8805ceb595dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571179} {:answer/id #uuid "bd39d6f9-1a79-5269-bb60-a3705107fe12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571183} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c9af5a-45a8-5973-9c44-186d59bb5f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571241} :answer-field/id #uuid "d76769ca-539d-5bea-bc37-7af743caadc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "48224f9c-af15-5271-930c-05fc20cc3fbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-107312" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n\nII. An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nIII. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ then $5v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =15.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{3}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $3$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=3v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(5v) &=5Av=5(3v)=3(5v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(5v)=3(5v),$ and $5v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $5v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3ceb3caa-5108-55c6-93e3-8d37c4a57f4a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000e3561-1544-5dc9-8c11-17c48b422a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284787} {:answer/id #uuid "097c3642-1c4d-5f84-8e55-34a351e7fb55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284868} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd337c9-0e8e-5c98-801f-ee9718e01ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284893} {:answer/id #uuid "201af8ed-db42-5e03-815b-dbf694b92e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285075} {:answer/id #uuid "fad2ed4b-35b0-525e-8240-933b2bc19e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285075} :answer-field/id #uuid "a631f2f2-9eef-524a-8c00-16323ea7e0c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac23d8ea-7894-5e1d-9e2b-923ff426fb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-18233" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $D$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Dv=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Dv & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} & =6\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-8c+140 \\\\ -7c+154\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6c \\\\ 84\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8c+140 &=6c \\\\ -7c+154 &=84\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-8c+140=6c\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7c+154=84\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=10.$"} :step/id #uuid "5e138194-7714-5b10-b6fc-e0340f52b114" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571511}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e12ff8-523f-5c8d-a706-ba8422caadff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8b688fcd0fd4e0eeee005687fcdc53fe96b671e551a99656df9cb77175f40df1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342321} {:answer/id #uuid "7af1fddd-ef2d-5429-8bef-94f40a541aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ba/baab64be7493281733cf8b4b94736d4bb46a43e511b379e939abf0cd3d822510.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343295} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9c7110-2ced-5415-9901-6bf2704afc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/75/75a8012b7823a0256e42a4db5fc40c3ea3a12226bd9989ab3de639c2ee271c98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343443} {:answer/id #uuid "d565df53-cdd1-579a-9144-4b2cc326733d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/af/afc3e8503656a84f23c7f58fd05e005ee07200b75ddec1aa5cf247b3484e81b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344108} {:answer/id #uuid "e502ad54-3f96-58bf-b350-a65382820455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2d/2dcc1cd1e1b93fa8b01e68e1bc102b5f76d83d954cbf0df11ecc7cc8a8e8dafb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344108} :answer-field/id #uuid "01840463-24e4-5419-b9c4-a0e3ada98130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87b8923c-30bf-57c2-9051-8311487777e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-75734" :question/problem "![](images/02/0269c9d60a97984f55cefccbd8cf1192dfdb4082935162206b1ce1a3cfb413f7.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-1.$ Find the vector $Bu.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Bu & =-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/af/afc3e8503656a84f23c7f58fd05e005ee07200b75ddec1aa5cf247b3484e81b4.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7931e841-3cfd-560d-84bf-f26f00e03321" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07eb1dc5-98f3-5daa-91e3-a9e09abb0895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570903} {:answer/id #uuid "37563492-e7cf-5fdf-9ea2-b0e40fe05275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570975} {:answer/id #uuid "8be08705-3f7e-5fdb-a167-e4244e9ea1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571105} {:answer/id #uuid "e4291a8f-63e2-5130-a4f3-66c8b39bc724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571245} {:answer/id #uuid "f462c8dd-3f8b-5230-9dc9-c062aa74de47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571105} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ef38cb-d22b-5c07-9abf-231f5e6be217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e4e6ce71-c74f-5790-9b61-f458926b26f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-18236" :question/problem "The eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3k+1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-k$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3k+1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =-k\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3k+16\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-k \\\\ -5k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=-k, \\\\ 3k+16 &=-5k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2=-k\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=-2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k+16 & =-5k \\\\ 3\\cdot (-2)+16 & =-5\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 10 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "e4278250-85d4-5e96-add4-c7d4c1187239" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd32804e-3923-5318-97f1-01f3adefac2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f1/f1e1b3eba7086332888fecf1c92642450a66c092b85a27b6284a482313e55394.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369012} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb6e91e-a3a5-5044-85bc-a35206a19811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7a/7a8d68549189fdeadfa6d12a668dd69e5fa545d33e992dcbd2c92fa44633959a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369013} {:answer/id #uuid "6de2baed-9e4a-5469-960d-0bd3b9f42f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/91/91d45713ecd57f376825f82a34efb2dcf7cede21a4085c46339193b26d86979d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369014} {:answer/id #uuid "eab22fda-9bb0-5ef0-9596-2ce1b90143fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/90/9056e7d00d0fd892f2281b534a59598db355cfd8caf9d239c552e609783091c8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369015} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2e4b1a-8072-5c8f-a594-1b08e4a5431f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bb/bb31aef8625f25f0048bb1f15f8c8fb75483e9d5fb5167b96398bcb4e25445d9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369016}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369015} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c18d877-b27b-546c-82e0-2263feeac823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2619eb87-a4d6-584a-ac06-7c0d135f7808" :question/math-academy-id "q-106876" :question/problem "![](images/95/952f35d03c8554f2feaeb50296df9ac426a49730a44038bf6f9cf47b1fa3f9ee.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\frac{5}{2}.$ Find the vector $Av.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{2}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\frac{5}{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/cb/cb3fae2e42e90e7e8398c311fd9c4d20ac1db51687a7d4f3f77146eb53728de5.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f02310af-9ad3-5d7c-90ac-a5a1f4308abf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571477}}] :activity/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570881}]}
